  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nCloudy and mild conditions continue across the Mid-South late\nthis morning. Cloud cover remains stubborn across the region,\ndriven by deep southwesterly flow aloft and persistent northeast\nwinds at the surface. Surface analysis places a 1017mb low over\nEast Tennessee with a quasi-stationary boundary extending\nsouthwest into central Mississippi and northern Alabama. Water\nvapor imagery shows a shortwave trough digging across central\nTexas with a broad trough progressing through the Northern\nPlains.\n\nExtensive cloudiness will persist through the weekend as\npersistent northeast surface flow keeps low-level moisture\ntrapped beneath a passing shortwave traversing the Lower\nMississippi Valley. Hi-res model guidance continues to indicate\nscattered post-frontal shower activity across northeast\nMississippi today and tonight driven by weak broad ascent. Areas\nfurther north should remain dry with temperatures holding steady\nin the mid 60s to lower 70s.\n\nAs the shortwave and northern stream longwave phase this evening,\nadditional isolated to scattered showers will spread across much\nof the Mid-South into Sunday morning. Rain chances will shift\nsouth and linger over northeast Mississippi through Sunday\nafternoon, where an isolated thunderstorm cannot be ruled out\nduring the peak heating hours. A reinforcing cold front will move\nthrough Sunday night, shunting moisture south, ending\nprecipitation, and ushering in a cooler, drier air mass.\n\nTemperatures will return near seasonal norms early next week,\nwith daily highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s and morning lows\nin the 50s under broad northwest flow aloft. Ensemble guidance\n(LREF) indicates a gradual warming trend through late week as a\nsubtropical ridge builds over the region. Highs will likely top\nout in the mid to upper 80s by Friday-roughly 5 to 8 degrees\nabove normal. Both deterministic GFS and ECMWF runs feature a cut-\noff low developing beneath the ridge by next weekend, which could\nbring a return of unsettled, wet weather to the area.",
